  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+2\\cdot 3\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+6\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+6\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
